# Measurement of Hypertriton Lifetime at STAR

Yifei Xu\({}^{1,2}\) for the STAR Collaboration

\({}^{1}\)Shanghai institute of applied physics, 2019 Jiaho Road, Jiading, Shanghai 201800, China

\({}^{2}\)Brookhaven National Laboratory, 2 Center Street, Upton, New York 11973, USA

E-mail: xuyifei@sinap.ac.cn

###### Abstract

A precise measurement of the hypertriton lifetime and the ratio between the branching ratios of its two main decay channels has been made through the two body and three body decay channels with data of Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm{cs}}}=7.7\), 11.5, 19.6, 27, 39 and 200 GeV at STAR. The hypertriton lifetime is \(155^{+25}_{-25}(stat.)\pm 31(syst.)\) ps with the combined channels. It is shorter than the lifetime of a free A and is consistent with recent results from HypHI experiment at GSI and ALICE experiment at LHC. The ratio of \(B.R._{\rm{(\frac{3}{A}\!H\to\!H\!e\to\!\pi)}}/B.R._{\rm{(\frac{3}{A}\!H\to\!d \!p\!+\!\pi^{+})}}\) is \(0.42^{+0.07}_{-0.06}(stat.)\pm 0.12(syst.)\). It is consistent with the early results from helium bubble chamber experiments.

hypertriton, hyperon-nucleon interaction, lifetime, decay branching ratio 021005/12

Yifei Xu\({}^{1,2}\) for the STAR Collaboration

\({}^{1}\)Shanghai institute of applied physics, 2019 Jiaho Road, Jiading, Shanghai 201800, China

\({}^{2}\)Brookhaven National Laboratory, 2 Center Street, Upton, New York 11973, USA

E-mail: xuyifei@sinap.ac.cn

## 1 Introduction

Since the first observation of hypernucleus by M. Danysz and J. Pniewski in 1952 [1], the study of hypernucleus became a subject of big interest by physicists. The hyperon nucleon (YN) interaction will provide insight into the QCD theory and will also help to understand the equation of state of high density matter such as the one in neutron stars [2]. Hypertriton, the lightest hypernucleus, can be abundantly produced in Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC), and is a good probe to study the YN interaction in laboratory. The lifetime of \({}^{3}_{\rm{A}}\)H is related to the strength of the YN interaction inside the nucleus [3, 4], and thus a precise measurement of \({}^{3}_{\rm{A}}\)H lifetime will help us understand the YN interaction [3, 5].

## 2 Datasets and Signal Reconstruction

The Solenoid Tracker At RHIC (STAR) [6] is a detector system that is located at RHIC of Brookhaven National Laboratory. The main detector of the STAR is Time Projection Chamber (TPC) [7]. The TPC provides momentum and energy loss information of outgoing particles generated in a collision. With this information, particles like \({}^{3}\)He, deuteron, proton, and \(\pi\) can be cleanly identified, which enables the reconstruction of the \({}^{3}_{\rm{A}}\)H through its two main channels : \({}^{3}_{\rm{A}}\)H \(\to\)\({}^{3}\)He + \(\pi^{-}\) and \({}^{3}\)H \(\to\) d + p + \(\pi^{-}\).

In this analysis, different datasets are used for two body and three body approaches. In the two body decay part, minimum-bias-trigger events from six different Au-Au collision energies, including 7.7 GeV, 11.5 GeV, 19.6 GeV, 27 GeV, 39 GeV and 200 GeV, are used for the \({}^{3}_{\rm{A}}\)H reconstruction. In the three body decay part, minimum-bias-trigger events from three Au-Au collision energies, including 27 GeV, 39 GeV and 200 GeV, are used. The primary vertex z position cut, the event number, and the statistics of \({}^{3}\)He daughters are summarized in Table 1. The counts of \({}^{3}_{\rm{A}}\)H, \({}^{3}\)He and \({}^{3}\)He candidates are also listed in the table. Only tracks with \(p_{t}>0.2\) GeV/c and pseudo-rapidity \(|\eta|<1.0\) are used in both analyses.

The \({}^{3}_{\rm{A}}\)H signals are reconstructed from the decays of daughters. The background distributionsare derived by rotating one of its daughters by 180 degrees in the azimuthal angle. For example, we rotated \({}^{3}\)He in the two body analysis and the deuteron in the three body channel analysis. This represents residual combinatorial background [8; 9]. The background was then fitted with a double exponential function: \(f(x)\propto exp(-\frac{x}{\rho_{1}})-exp(-\frac{x}{\rho_{2}})\), where \(x\) is the invariant mass, \(p_{1}\) and \(p_{2}\) are two individual parameters. The \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H candidates are derived from the bin-by-bin counts in the mass range [2.986,2.994] GeV/c\({}^{2}\) after subtraction of the residual combinatorial background. To extract the \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H signals, a group of topological cuts, including the decay length of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H, the closest distance (DCA) of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H to the primary vertex and the DCA between daughters, are applied. These cuts are optimized separately for the two body and the three body analyses based on the detector response simulation data.

## 3 Lifetime and Ratio

The decay of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H obeys the radioactive decay law: \(N(t)=N_{0}e^{-t/\tau}=N_{0}e^{-t/(\beta p\tau\tau)}\), where \(t\) stands for the \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H decay length. We reconstruct the \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H signals in four \(l/\beta\gamma\) bins for the two body analysis and only three bins for the three body analysis due to fewer counts. Then the yields of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H are corrected by the reconstruction efficiency and detector acceptance. The yields are normalized by the number of events, which is the same number as in the yield fitting for both channels. The lifetime parameter can be derived by fitting the data points of the two body and three body analyses together. The minimum \(\chi^{2}\) method is used for the determination of the lifetime. In Figure 1, we show the yield of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H in its two decay channels on the left panel. The right panel shows the \(\chi^{2}\) distribution of the fitting. As a result, the measured lifetime is \(155^{+25}_{-22}(stat.)\) ps.

As we can reconstruct \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H in its two main decay channels: \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H \(\rightarrow^{3}\)He + \(\pi^{-}\) and \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H \(\rightarrow\) d + p + \(\pi^{-}\) at the same time in the STAR experiment, it's possible to measure the ratio between the decay branching ratios of the two channels. The parameter \(N_{0}\) is derived from the yield fitting of both channels. Then we can calculate the ratio as \(ratio=B.R._{(^{3}_{\Lambda}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow^{3}\mathrm{He}+\pi^{-})}/B.R._{(^{ 3}_{\Lambda}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow\mathrm{d}+\mathrm{p}+\pi^{-})}\), and the result is \(0.42^{+0.07}_{-0.06}(stat.)\).

We considered two sources of the systematic uncertainties for the lifetime and branching ratio measurement: 1) The choice of bin width. The original analyses are using a bin width of 4 MeV, and we here use a bin width of 2 MeV for systematic uncertainty estimation. 2) The choice of the topological cuts: for the two body analysis, the decay length of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H, the DCA of \(\pi^{-}\) to the primary vertex, the DCA between daughters and the DCA of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H to the primary vertex are considered; for the three body analysis, the DCA between proton and pion, and the DCA between proton-pion pair to the primary vertex are considered. Within these two kinds of contributions considered, the systematic error for the lifetime is 31 ps, and it is 0.12 for the decay branching ratio.

As a further cross-check on the analysis procedure, the \(\Lambda\) is reconstructed via the \(\Lambda\to p+\pi^{-}\)decay channel in the same data set. We use exactly the same method to obtain the \(\Lambda\) lifetime and the result is \(260\pm 1\) ps. It is well consistent with the \(\tau=263\pm 2\) ps compiled by the Particle Data Group [10].

## 4 Discussion and Summary

Since 1960s, there have been several measurements for the lifetime of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H [8, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18]. We draw a summary plot in Fig 2. For a long time, the \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H is believed to be a weakly bound state of a \(\Lambda\) and a deuteron, so the lifetime of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H should be close to that of a free \(\Lambda\). From Fig 2, we can find that some of the measurements [12, 13, 15, 16] show lifetime values close to that of \(\Lambda\), while others [8, 11, 14, 17, 18] show shorter lifetime value than the \(\Lambda\). The measurements in [12, 13, 14, 16] used nuclear emulsion technique. Their results are close to the \(\Lambda\) lifetime except for [14]. They discussed that this may be due to dissociation of the lightly bound \(\Lambda\) and deuteron when traveling in a dense medium. The measurement in [11] used helium bubble chamber, and the result was also very short with large statistical uncertainty. Since 2010, there are several new measurements from STAR [8], GSI [17] and ALICE [18]. Their results are consistent with each other and all show a shorter lifetime than the free \(\Lambda\). Saito and his colleagues have carried out a statistical combination of the available lifetime measurements and concluded that the lifetime of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H could be shorter than the \(\Lambda\)'s [19]. Our measurement indicates that the interaction between a \(\Lambda\) and a neutron may be stronger than expected [4] and further theoretical study is required.

The branching ratio of \(\pi\) mesonic decay of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H is considered to be closely related to its spin assignment [20]. Our measurement is in agreement with the mean value of helium bubble chamber results \(0.35\pm 0.04\)[16]. It is a little lower than the value obtained from high statistical measurements based on the nuclear emulsion technique, where the author claimed a spin assignment of 1/2 is confirmed in their data [21]. More theoretical understanding on the nature of the \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H is needed.

In summary, we present here an improved result of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H lifetime as \(155^{+25}_{-22}(stat.)\pm 31(syst.)\) ps. We also present the ratio between the branching ratios of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H, i.e. \(B.R._{(^{3}_{\Lambda}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow^{3}\mathrm{He}+\pi^{-})}/B.R._{(^{3}_ {\Lambda}\mathrm{H}\rightarrow\mathrm{d}\mathrm{e}+\pi^{-})}\), to be _ratio_ =\(0.42^{+0.07}_{-0.06}(stat.)\pm 0.12(syst.)\). Our lifetime value is shorter than the free \(\Lambda\)'s, which calls for further theoretical study of \({}^{3}_{\Lambda}\)H structure.

Figure 1: The yield of hypertriton for both the two body and the three body channel analyses. In the left panel: the red dots indicate the two body channel and the blue boxes indicate the three body channel. The yield has already been corrected by theoretical branching ratios (40.15% for the three body channel and 24.88% for the two body channel [3]). The right panel shows the \(\chi^{2}\) distribution of lifetime fitting with the minimum \(\chi^{2}\) method. The distribution shows the best estimation of the lifetime is \(155^{+25}_{-22}(stat.)\) ps.

